<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The Apple Push Notification service environment this build talks to.

	  `development` is correct for every build made from Xcode and for
	  TestFlight internal testing; the App Store re-signs the binary for
	  production when it is uploaded, so shipping `development` here is the
	  normal arrangement rather than a mistake. A build signed for one
	  environment and sent a token minted in the other silently receives
	  nothing, which is the single commonest reason iOS push "does not work".

	  Without this entitlement the app cannot register with APNs at all, and
	  Firebase then has no device token to exchange for an FCM one — so
	  `getToken()` returns null for ever and the server never learns this
	  handset exists.

	  This file has to be attached to the Runner target in Xcode
	  (Signing & Capabilities -> + Capability -> Push Notifications), which
	  also requires the bundle id `shop.reem.app` to have the Push
	  Notifications capability enabled in the Apple Developer portal, and an
	  APNs authentication key uploaded to the Firebase console under
	  Cloud Messaging -> Apple app configuration. All three, or nothing
	  arrives.
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>

	<!--
	  Universal Links — an https link that opens the app instead of Safari.

	  iOS fetches `https://<domain>/.well-known/apple-app-site-association` from
	  each domain listed here, through Apple's own CDN, and checks that it names
	  this app's `TEAMID.shop.reem.app`. The API serves that file (see
	  `backend/src/modules/links`), generated from IOS_APP_IDS — until that
	  variable is set it is an empty association, no link is claimed, and every
	  link opens the web page instead. Nothing breaks either way.

	  Two things this needs that a file cannot supply, both one-time:

	   - Associated Domains must be enabled for the App ID in the Apple
	     Developer portal, and this file attached to the Runner target in Xcode
	     (Signing & Capabilities). Without both, the entitlement is dropped at
	     signing and links silently stop opening the app.
	   - Apple's CDN caches the association file. Get IOS_APP_IDS right before
	     the first TestFlight build goes out, or expect a wait.

	  Both hosts are listed so that moving to the custom domain is a deploy
	  rather than a release: links already in circulation on the onrender.com
	  host keep opening the app. `?mode=developer` is deliberately absent — it
	  bypasses the CDN and is a debugging aid, not a shipping configuration.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:reem-api.onrender.com</string>
		<string>applinks:reem.shop</string>
		<string>applinks:www.reem.shop</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
